<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Front for the Liberation of the Enclave of Cabinda</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1963">1963</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as FLEC</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Front for the Liberation of the Enclave of Cabinda (FLEC) is a separatist movement seeking independence for Cabinda, the oil-rich Angolan exclave north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congo-river">Congo River</a>. Founded in 1963 from earlier Cabindan movements, it claimed independence in 1975 but was excluded from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alvor-agreement">Alvor Agreement</a>, which made Cabinda part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angola">Angola</a>. Its factions have kept up a low-level armed struggle since then.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/flec.jpg" alt="Front for the Liberation of the Enclave of Cabinda" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Saippuakauppias · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:COA_Republic_of_Cabinda.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-simulambuco">Treaty of Simulambuco</a>, signed on 1 February 1885, made Cabinda a Portuguese protectorate, which was at once linked to the much larger colony of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angola">Angola</a>. Cabindan separatists such as the FLEC maintain that the treaty recognised the territory's special status. In 1956 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estado-novo-portugal">Estado Novo</a> of António Salazar made Cabinda an integral part of Angola, shortly after a reminder from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> that Portugal had an obligation to report on its self-governing territories.</p><p>In 1963 three of the groups seeking independence, the Movement for the Liberation of the Enclave of Cabinda, the Action Committee of the Cabinda National Union and the Mayombe National Alliance, merged to form the FLEC at a conference in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pointe-noire">Pointe-Noire</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/republic-of-the-congo">Congo-Brazzaville</a>. It was the main liberation movement but not the only one, competing for support with the Movement for the Liberation of Cabinda and the Union of Cabindan People.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>With the independence of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angola">Angola</a> in 1975, Cabinda became an exclave province of the new state, and the FLEC fought what it calls the Cabinda War in the region of the former kingdoms of Kakongo, Loango and N'Goyo. The movement broke into factions, among them FLEC-Ranque Franque, FLEC-N'Zita under Henrique N'zita Tiago and FLEC-Lubota under Francisco Xavier Lubota, with further groups formed in 1977, 1979 and 1996. In December 2002 the Angolan Armed Forces announced the capture of FLEC-Renovada, and in October 2006 FLEC-FAC asked the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/african-union">African Union</a>'s Commission on Human and Peoples' Rights to intervene.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cold-war">Cold War</a> the FLEC received support from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mobutu-sese-seko">Mobutu Sese Seko</a> of neighbouring <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaire">Zaire</a>, and limited support from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gabon">Gabon</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>; these supporters sought Cabinda's independence to secure oil rights. In the 1980s it also had help from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unita">UNITA</a> and from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>. In May 2000 FLEC-FAC kidnapped three foreign employees and one local employee of a Portuguese contractor, who were released within two months. On 8 January 2010 gunmen attacked the bus of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/togo">Togo</a> national football team in Cabinda, killing the assistant coach, the team spokesman and the driver. An offshoot of the FLEC claimed responsibility; its spokesman said the attack had been aimed at the Angolan escort.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Front for the Liberation of the Enclave of Cabinda” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Separatists of the <b>Front for the Liberation of the Enclave of Cabinda</b> claimed independence, but the 1975 Alvor Agreement made Cabinda part of independent Angola, where it remains a province with a low-level separatist conflict.</blockquote><a href="/state/portuguese-congo">Portuguese Congo</a></li></ul></section>
